.TH GIT-PULLKEEP 1 "2026-11-06" "git-pullkeep 0.1.0" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-pullkeep \- pull with uncommitted changes, parking only the files that block it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit pullkeep\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fI<remote>\fR [\fI<branch>\fR]] [\fIgit-pull options\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBgit pull\fR refuses to run when a locally modified file is also modified upstream.
\fBgit pullkeep\fR parks only those overlapping files in a temporary commit, runs the pull,
then collapses the temporary and merge commits again with \fBgit reset --mixed\fR.
Every other dirty file never leaves the working tree, the index (staged) state is restored,
and no stash is created. In merge mode, staged entries outside the overlap are parked too:
git mergeR refuses to start while the index differs from \fBHEAD\fR, so they block the
pull exactly like overlapping edits. In \fB\-\-rebase\fR mode every dirty tracked file
(including staged ones) is parked, because rebase itself requires a clean tree.
.PP
A snapshot of the pre-pull working tree is stored under
\fBrefs/pullkeep/<stamp>\fR so \fB\-\-undo\fR can restore it exactly.
Untracked files whose path is claimed by upstream are moved to
\fB.git/pullkeep-aside/<stamp>/\fR and restored by \fB\-\-undo\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-dry-run\fR
Analyse and report only: no working-tree, index or history change. The pre-flight fetch still
runs unless \fB\-\-no-fetch\fR is given.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Exit 10 if the pull would have to park or resolve files, 0 otherwise. Intended for CI.
.TP
\fB\-\-continue\fR, \fB\-\-finish\fR
After conflicts were resolved and committed (merge) or after \fBgit rebase \-\-continue\fR,
collapse the temporary/merge commits back into uncommitted changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-abort\fR, \fB\-\-undo\fR
Restore the working tree, index and branch from the most recent snapshot.
Commits created after the run are discarded. Requires confirmation; use \fB\-y\fR when
stdin is not a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-recover\fR
Collapse a temporary commit left behind by an interrupted run (conflicts, killed shell).
The target is only chosen when it is unambiguous: the upstream tip if it is already merged
into \fBHEAD\fR, otherwise the parent of the temporary commit. Otherwise the command exits
with status 20 and prints instructions. Does nothing (status 0) when no temporary commit is
there.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-fetch\fR
Skip the pre-flight fetch and analyse against the current remote-tracking ref.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep-backups=\fIN\fR
Keep the \fIN\fR most recent snapshots under \fBrefs/pullkeep/\fR (default 5).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show usage. Note that \fBgit pullkeep \-\-help\fR is intercepted by git itself and looks for
this man page, which is why \fB\-h\fR is the reliable form.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.SH RELAYED GIT-PULL OPTIONS
\fB\-\-rebase\fR[=\fImerges\fR], \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-no-rebase\fR,
\fB\-\-no-ff\fR, \fB\-\-ff\fR, \fB\-X\fR \fIoption\fR, \fB\-s\fR \fIstrategy\fR,
\fB\-\-no-edit\fR, \fB\-\-edit\fR, \fB\-\-signoff\fR, \fB\-\-stat\fR, \fB\-\-no-stat\fR,
\fB\-\-summary\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR, \fB\-\-progress\fR,
\fB\-\-prune\fR, \fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-tags\fR, \fB\-\-no-tags\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR,
\fB\-\-force\fR, \fB\-\-depth=\fIN\fR, \fB\-\-deepen=\fIN\fR, \fB\-\-jobs=\fIN\fR.
.PP
Because rebase requires a clean tree, \fB\-\-rebase\fR parks \fIall\fR dirty tracked files
instead of only the overlapping ones; they are restored after the pull.
.SH REJECTED OPTIONS
\fB\-\-ff-only\fR (parking files requires a local commit, so the update cannot be a
fast-forward), \fB\-\-squash\fR and \fB\-\-no-commit\fR (no commit layer to collapse),
\fB\-\-allow-unrelated-histories\fR (no merge base, so no overlap analysis),
\fB\-\-rebase=interactive\fR (needs a terminal). These exit with status 21.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success, or already up to date.
.TP
2
Bad command line: unknown option, missing value, or \fB\-\-undo\fR without \fB\-y\fR when
stdin is not a terminal.
.TP
10
\fB\-\-check\fR found files that would have to be parked or resolved.
.TP
20
The run stopped and needs a human: merge/rebase conflicts, or \fBgit pull\fR itself failed.
Your local changes are still intact; use \fB\-\-continue\fR, \fB\-\-recover\fR or
\fB\-\-undo\fR.
.TP
21
Rejected option combination.
.TP
30
Repository or environment problem: not a repo, detached HEAD, no upstream, the fetch
failed, \fBuser.name\fR/\fBuser.email\fR are not configured, or there is no snapshot to undo.
.TP
31
An unfinished merge, rebase, cherry-pick or revert is in progress, or a temporary commit
from an earlier run is still reachable from \fBHEAD\fR (run \fB\-\-continue\fR,
\fB\-\-recover\fR or \fB\-\-undo\fR).
.TP
35
The repository is locked: another pullkeep run is already writing to it. Wait for that run;
if you are sure it is gone, delete \fB\$GIT_COMMON_DIR/pullkeep.lock\fR.
.TP
40
Internal error; a snapshot ref that was already created is preserved for \fB\-\-undo\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git pullkeep                       # merge, keep everything uncommitted
git pullkeep \-\-rebase              # rebase, park all dirty files
git pullkeep \-\-dry-run             # what would happen?
git pullkeep \-\-check; echo $?       # 0 = plain pull is enough
git pullkeep origin main           # explicit remote and branch
git pullkeep \-\-continue            # after resolving conflicts
git pullkeep \-\-recover             # collapse a temp commit from a dead run
git pullkeep \-\-undo \-y             # exact rollback
.fi
.SH SAFETY
Temporary commits are labelled \fBwip: pullkeep temp commit\fR and carry a
\fBpullkeep-temp:\fR trailer (stamp, base, upstream, strategy); the same facts are stored in
\fB\$GIT_DIR/pullkeep-meta/\fR. Before doing anything, the command scans the last 50 commits
reachable from \fBHEAD\fR (override with \fBPULLKEEP_SCAN\fR) and refuses with status 31 if it
finds one, so a second run can never stack on top of the first. Consequently a run that is
repeated on an up-to-date branch is a no-op, and an interrupted run never turns the marker
commit into permanent history.
.PP
Only one writing run is allowed per repository: \fBrun\fR, \fB\-\-continue\fR,
\fB\-\-recover\fR and \fB\-\-undo\fR take a lock in the common git dir (a \fBmkdir\fR, so no
\fBflock\fR(1) is needed, and linked worktrees of the same repository share it). A lock left
by a dead process is reclaimed. \fB\-\-dry-run\fR and \fB\-\-check\fR never take it.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgit-pull\fR(1), \fBgit-stash\fR(1), \fBgit-reset\fR(1), \fBgit-merge\fR(1), \fBgit-rebase\fR(1)
